@page
@model CSharpMasteryHub.Pages.SqlServer.IndexesPerformanceModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Indexes & Performance";
}

<div class="container">
    <div class="row">
        <div class="col-lg-9">
            <nav aria-label="breadcrumb">
                <ol class="breadcrumb">
                    <li class="breadcrumb-item"><a href="/">Home</a></li>
                    <li class="breadcrumb-item"><a href="/SqlServer">SQL Server</a></li>
                    <li class="breadcrumb-item active">Indexes & Performance</li>
                </ol>
            </nav>

            <div class="lesson-content">
                <h1 class="display-4 mb-3">Indexes &amp; Performance</h1>
                <div class="d-flex gap-3 mb-4">
                    <span class="badge bg-danger">Advanced</span>
                    <span class="badge bg-info text-dark">25 min read</span>
                    <span class="badge bg-primary">Lesson 9 of 10</span>
                </div>

                <p>
                    An index lets SQL Server find rows without scanning the entire table —
                    similar to a book's index letting you skip straight to a page.
                </p>

                <h2>Creating an index</h2>
                <pre><code>CREATE INDEX IX_Students_LastName
ON Students (LastName);</code></pre>
                <p>Now queries filtering or sorting on <code>LastName</code> are much faster on large tables.</p>

                <h2>Composite index</h2>
                <pre><code>CREATE INDEX IX_Students_LastFirst
ON Students (LastName, FirstName);</code></pre>
                <p>Useful when you frequently filter/sort by both columns together, in that order.</p>

                <h2>Primary keys are indexed automatically</h2>
                <p>
                    You don't need to manually index a <code>PRIMARY KEY</code> column —
                    SQL Server creates a clustered index for it automatically.
                </p>

                <h2>Checking query performance</h2>
                <pre><code>SET STATISTICS IO ON;
SELECT * FROM Students WHERE LastName = 'Ahmed';</code></pre>
                <p>Look at the "logical reads" in the Messages tab — fewer reads generally means a faster query.</p>

                <h2>When NOT to over-index</h2>
                <ul>
                    <li>Every index speeds up reads but slows down INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE (the index must be maintained too).</li>
                    <li>Don't index columns you rarely filter or sort by.</li>
                    <li>Don't index small tables — a full scan is already fast.</li>
                </ul>

                <div class="card bg-info bg-opacity-10 mb-4">
                    <div class="card-body">
                        <h5 class="card-title"><i class="fas fa-key me-2"></i>Key Takeaway</h5>
                        <p>Indexes speed up reads but slow down writes. Create indexes on columns you frequently filter or sort by.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="mt-5 d-flex justify-content-between">
                    <a href="/SqlServer/08-FunctionsAndViews" class="btn btn-outline-primary">
                        <i class="fas fa-arrow-left me-2"></i>Previous Lesson
                    </a>
                    <a href="/SqlServer/10-Transactions" class="btn btn-primary-custom">
                        Next Lesson <i class="fas fa-arrow-right ms-2"></i>
                    </a>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="col-lg-3">
            @await Html.PartialAsync("_SidebarNav", Model.SidebarNav)
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

@await Html.PartialAsync("_QuizButton", "SqlServer_Lesson9")